  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has 39 fields. The time field is `timestamp`. The `response` field is a text field with a `response.keyword` sub-field.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-af3f07d7 Web logs' (ID `419899ae-a18e-4391-8332-fadbe89d2a76`) has two XY panels on the data view, using `timestamp` as the time field. One is a line chart of request count over time. The other is a bar chart of count by `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-af3f07d7 5xx errors' (ID `31fa723d-e518-489b-a334-51b131a9c37e`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every 1m and counts documents over a 5m window where `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\". It fires when the count is greater than 10. I created it enabled, so no separate enable call was needed.\n4. **Rename:** The response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. The rule read back with `enabled: true` and `execution_status: ok`.\n\nMy choices:\n- I filtered on `response.keyword` as a string range, not a numeric one. The codes are three-digit strings, so string order matches numeric order. I couldn't run ES|QL queries here, so I didn't check the counts against the data. The rule's `ok` status only shows it ran without error.\n- I used the data view's `timestamp` field for the rule and the panels. The index also has an `@timestamp` field, which I didn't use.\n\nRULE_ENABLED is read back from the API, not inferred from the create call.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
